>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Protein Powder for Vegetarians</Fragment>
      Vegetarians are not limited to pea and rice. Whey, casein and egg white are all on the table, which makes the choice wider and the shake better, as long as you know how the whey was made and what else is hiding in the tub.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#wider-field">Why the Field Is Wider</a>
      <a href="#rennet">The Rennet Question</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#brand-statements">What Brands Say</a>
      <a href="#leucine">Leucine Gaps</a>
      <a href="#egg-white">Egg White</a>
      <a href="#hidden-ingredients">Gelatin and Collagen</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#daily-plan">A Vegetarian Protein Day</a>
      <a href="#which-type">Which Type to Choose</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Most protein powder advice for vegetarians is really advice for vegans. It sends you straight to pea, rice and hemp blends and treats dairy and eggs as if they were off the menu. For a lacto-ovo vegetarian they are not. Whey, casein and egg white are all meat-free, all complete proteins, and all carry more leucine per gram than most plant powders. The real questions for a vegetarian are narrower and more specific: was the whey made from cheese set with animal rennet, does the tub hide gelatin or collagen, and if you eat little dairy, is your plant protein doing enough? This guide answers those three questions first, then ranks six powders across whey, casein, egg and plant on what the labels say and what 25 g of protein actually costs.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="wider-field" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Vegetarians Have More Options Than Vegans
          </h2>

          <p>
            A vegetarian diet excludes the flesh of animals: meat, poultry, fish and shellfish, plus ingredients made from them, such as gelatin and animal-derived collagen. A lacto-ovo vegetarian still eats dairy and eggs. A lacto-vegetarian eats dairy but not eggs, and an ovo-vegetarian eats eggs but not dairy. Each of those groups has a wider protein powder shelf than a vegan does, and that matters more than most buying guides admit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey and casein are the two proteins in milk. Egg white protein is dried egg albumen. All three are complete proteins, meaning they supply every essential amino acid in useful amounts, and all three score at or near the top of the digestibility scales used to grade protein quality. A vegan has to work around the lower leucine and slightly lower digestibility of plant proteins with bigger servings and blends. A vegetarian can simply choose a dairy or egg powder and skip that problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not make plant protein a bad choice for vegetarians. Pea, soy and blended plant powders are useful if you are lactose intolerant, avoid eggs, eat mostly plants for environmental reasons, or simply prefer the taste. Our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide ranks them in depth for anyone eating fully vegan. This page is different: it treats whey, casein, egg and plant as one field and asks which is best for someone who eats dairy and eggs but no meat.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is that dairy proteins are not automatically vegetarian in the strict sense. Most whey is a by-product of cheese making, and some cheese is set with rennet taken from the stomachs of calves. Many vegetarians accept whey regardless; some do not. Separately, flavored powders and protein products sometimes include gelatin, collagen or bone broth, which no vegetarian definition allows. Those two issues, not protein quality, are what make choosing a vegetarian powder harder than it looks.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the ranking below rewards three things. First, a protein source whose vegetarian status is clear, either because the brand says so or because the way it is made rules out animal enzymes. Second, enough leucine per serving to do the job a protein shake is usually bought for. Third, a fair price per 25 g of protein, the dose most people take in one shake.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rennet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Rennet Question in Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rennet is a set of enzymes that curdles milk. In cheese making it splits casein so the milk sets into curds, and the liquid left over is whey. That whey is then filtered, concentrated and dried into whey protein concentrate or isolate. If the cheese was made with traditional animal rennet, which comes from the stomach lining of young calves, then the whey is a by-product of a process that used an animal-slaughter ingredient.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whether that makes whey non-vegetarian is a matter of personal standard rather than chemistry. The rennet enzymes act on the curd and are not added to the whey powder as an ingredient, and Naked Nutrition, for one, makes exactly that point in its own guide. Strict vegetarians, and groups such as the Vegetarian Resource Group, argue that the source of the enzyme still matters and that companies should disclose it. Both positions are reasonable. What is not reasonable is assuming every whey is fine or every whey is off limits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Animal rennet is no longer the default. Most commercial cheese is now set with coagulants that do not come from slaughter: microbial rennet produced by fungi, or fermentation-produced chymosin, which is the calf enzyme made by microorganisms. Whey from cheese made either way is generally considered vegetarian. The trouble is that a whey powder label almost never says which coagulant was used, and a single brand may buy whey from several cheese plants.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two ways around the question</h3>

          <p>
            Some dairy proteins never pass through a cheese vat at all. Native whey and micellar casein are usually separated directly from skim milk by filtration, so no coagulant is involved. That is why casein ranks well here, and why native whey products are worth a look, though many blend native whey with conventional whey, so check the full ingredient list rather than the front of the tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other route is the brand statement. A few companies answer the question in writing. Momentous says in its help center that rennet is not used in producing its grass-fed whey, which is why it tops our list for vegetarians who want certainty. In India, whey sold with the green FSSAI vegetarian mark has been declared vegetarian under that system, and Optimum Nutrition's support pages point to that mark for its India-made Gold Standard. A US label with no statement is not proof of animal rennet; it is simply an unanswered question you can email the brand about.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Protein Powders for Vegetarians
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these on whether the protein source fits a lacto-ovo vegetarian diet without guesswork, what the brand says about enzymes or processing, protein and leucine per serving, documented third-party testing and approximate cost per 25 g of protein. Label figures are per the label or the brand's listing at the time of writing and vary by flavor; prices were checked on November 1, 2026 and move often. We have not tasted or lab-tested these powders ourselves. Vegetarian status is a brand statement or a consequence of how the protein is made, not an independent certification, and we say which is which for each pick.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="brand-statements" class="scroll-mt-24">
