                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NOW Sports Egg White, NOW Sports Soy, Truvani</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Egg or plant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No cheese making involved</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Silence is the most common answer, and it is not a red flag on its own. Transparent Labs, Legion and Klean Athlete make well-tested whey isolates that we rate elsewhere on the site, but we could not find a published rennet statement for any of them. If that matters to you, email customer service and ask two questions: what coagulant was used in the cheese your whey came from, and is that consistent across suppliers. A clear written answer is worth more than a vegetarian badge with no explanation behind it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be wary of secondary sources. Searching for a brand name plus "rennet" turns up auto-generated pages that confidently state a brand uses microbial or fungal rennet, often contradicting themselves from one paragraph to the next. None of them cite the manufacturer. We left those claims out, and you should too, unless you can trace them to the brand itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certifications are a separate question. NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport and Informed Choice test for banned substances and label accuracy. They do not certify that a product is vegetarian, and a tested whey can still come from animal-rennet cheese. Vegan certification on a plant powder does settle the question, because a vegan product cannot contain any animal-derived ingredient.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you would rather not research this at all, the practical shortcut is to pick a protein that never touched a cheese vat: egg white, micellar casein, native whey or a plant powder. Every pick on our list is chosen so that, at minimum, you know which category it sits in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="leucine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Leucine: Where Plant-Only Diets Fall Short
          </h2>

          <p>
            Leucine is the amino acid that switches on muscle protein synthesis. Research on whey suggests that roughly 2 to 3 g of leucine in a meal is enough to get most of the effect in younger adults, with older adults needing the higher end. That is why protein powders are compared on leucine, not just on grams of protein. Two powders with 25 g of protein each can deliver very different doses.
          </p>

          <p>
            The labels show the spread. Per 25 g of protein, Momentous whey works out to about 3.1 g of leucine, Naked Whey about 3.0 g, Naked Egg, a plain egg white we did not rank here, about 2.3 g, and Naked Casein about 2.3 g. NOW Sports Egg White reaches about 3.0 g only because it adds BCAAs. On the plant side, NOW Sports Soy comes to about 2.3 g and NOW Sports Pea about 2.1 g; Truvani does not publish a leucine figure. These are label figures that vary by flavor and batch, but the pattern holds: dairy leads, egg and soy sit in the middle, and pea and blends trail slightly.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Leucine per 25 g of Protein (label math)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3.1 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Momentous whey</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3.0 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Naked Whey</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2.3 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Naked Egg</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2.1 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Garden of Life Sport</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For a vegetarian who eats plenty of dairy and eggs, this gap barely matters, because the rest of the diet covers it. It matters more for vegetarians who eat mostly plants and use only plant protein, and for older adults, whose muscles respond less to a given dose. Plant-heavy vegetarian diets can also run lower in total protein than people assume, because beans, lentils and grains carry protein alongside a lot of carbohydrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is simple and does not require switching sources. If you use a plant powder, take a slightly bigger serving, around 30 to 35 g of protein rather than 25 g, which brings most pea and soy powders to roughly 2.5 g of leucine. Or split the difference with a scoop of whey or egg in your plant shake. Our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> comparison walks through the evidence that, at matched leucine, plant and whey build muscle about equally well.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this is medical advice. If you are managing a kidney condition, a diagnosed deficiency or a specific clinical diet, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="egg-white" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Egg White: The Rennet-Free Animal Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Egg white protein is underrated by vegetarians. It is a complete protein, it carries a respectable 2.3 g or so of leucine per 25 g, it contains no lactose, and it involves no cheese making, so the rennet question simply does not arise. For a lacto-ovo vegetarian who wants an animal-quality protein without any enzyme ambiguity, it is the most straightforward choice on the shelf.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-offs are taste and texture. Unflavored egg white is flat and a little sulfurous to some palates, and it whips into foam if you shake it hard. It mixes best into a blender smoothie, oats, pancakes or baking, where its tendency to set works in your favor. Flavored egg white powders exist, but they add sweeteners and flavors, which brings back the label-reading you were trying to avoid.
          </p>

          <p>
            Price is closer to whey than people expect. NOW Sports Egg White, our pick, works out to roughly $2.13 per 25 g of protein at the price we saw, and Naked Egg, a two-ingredient alternative, to about $1.82, almost exactly the same as Naked Whey. NOW uses a small 15 g serving with added BCAAs, so compare per gram of protein rather than per scoop. Our <a href="/best-egg-white-protein-powder/">best egg white protein powder</a> guide ranks six options on label and cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Egg white is also the obvious pick for vegetarians who are lactose intolerant or allergic to milk. Whey isolate is low in lactose but not free of milk protein, so it is no use to anyone with a true dairy allergy. Egg is, of course, a common allergen in its own right, so it is not a universal solution.
          </p>

          <p>
            One thing egg white does not do well is satiety in a liquid shake. It digests at a moderate pace, faster than casein, and a 110-calorie shake does not hold most people for long. Pair it with fruit, oats or nut butter if you use it as a breakfast rather than a post-workout top-up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hidden-ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Gelatin, Collagen and Other Hidden Animal Ingredients
          </h2>

          <p>
            The rennet question gets the attention, but the more clear-cut problem for vegetarians is ingredients that are made directly from slaughtered animals. Gelatin and most collagen are produced from the skin, bones and connective tissue of cattle, pigs or fish. Bone broth protein is made from bones. None of them are vegetarian under any definition, and all of them turn up in protein products more often than you might think.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen peptides are sold as a protein powder in their own right and are sometimes blended into whey or plant formulas to raise the protein number cheaply. Collagen is also an incomplete protein, low in leucine and missing tryptophan, so it does not contribute much to muscle building even for those who eat it. If a vegetarian label lists hydrolyzed collagen, bovine collagen, marine collagen or bone broth anywhere in the ingredients, put it back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gelatin shows up less in powders and more in the products around them: protein bars with a chewy layer, protein gummies, marshmallow-style coatings and some capsules. If you are shopping for bars as well as powder, our sibling guide to the <a href="/best-protein-bars/">best protein bars</a> is a good place to compare labels, and the same rule applies: read past the protein line to the full ingredient list.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Smaller ingredients worth a second look</h3>

          <p>
            A few minor ingredients come from animals and cause disagreement. Carmine, also listed as cochineal or natural red 4, is a red coloring made from insects and appears in some strawberry and berry flavors. Vitamin D3 in fortified meal shakes is often made from lanolin, which comes from sheep's wool, and in some products from fish oil. Omega-3 added to meal replacements may be fish oil. Most vegetarians accept lanolin but not fish or insects; check with the brand if a fortified product does not say.
          </p>

          <p>
            Natural flavors are the hardest to judge, because they can legally come from animal sources without saying so. A product carrying a vegetarian or vegan certification has had that checked. Single-ingredient unflavored powders, like most of our picks, remove the question entirely, which is one reason we favor them for vegetarians.
          </p>
        </div>

