        <div id="daily-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Building a Vegetarian Protein Day
          </h2>

          <p>
            A protein powder is a top-up, not a diet. Most active adults do well on roughly 1.2 to 1.6 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day, spread across three or four meals of 25 to 40 g each. For a 70 kg person that is about 85 to 110 g a day. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> will give you a personal figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lacto-ovo vegetarians usually find the target easier than they expect. Two eggs and a cup of Greek yogurt at breakfast, a lentil and grain bowl with cheese at lunch, tofu or tempeh at dinner, and a single shake add up fast. The meals that fall short tend to be pasta, rice and vegetable dishes without a concentrated protein, and that is where a scoop earns its place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match the powder to the meal. Whey suits the hour after training because it digests quickly and has the most leucine. Casein suits the evening, stirred into yogurt or made into a thick pudding, because it digests slowly. Egg white suits breakfast cooking, from protein pancakes to oats. Plant powders suit smoothies with fruit and greens, where their earthier taste is masked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixing sources is a legitimate strategy, not a compromise. A smoothie with half a scoop of whey and half a scoop of pea protein gets most of whey's leucine and most of pea's lower cost. A bowl of oats made with egg white and topped with Greek yogurt covers two complete proteins in one meal. Vegetarians have the advantage of being able to do this; use it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are pregnant, breastfeeding, managing diabetes or kidney disease, or feeding a child or teenager, protein needs and safe supplements differ. Ask your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a powder, and see our guides for those specific situations rather than relying on general targets.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Type of Protein Powder Should a Vegetarian Choose?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you eat dairy, are not bothered by the rennet question and want the most muscle-building value per dollar, choose a single-ingredient whey concentrate like Naked Whey. It carries close to 3 g of leucine per 25 g of protein at under $2 per serving. If your tolerance for lactose is low, step up to an isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you eat dairy but want the rennet question answered, choose a whey whose brand states no animal rennet is used, as Momentous does, or choose micellar casein, which is normally made without cheese at all. Casein is the cheaper of the two; whey is the better post-workout shake. You can also email any whey brand you like and ask, and keep the written answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you eat eggs, are sensitive to dairy, or simply want an animal protein with nothing to decode, choose an unflavored egg white like NOW Sports Egg White, or Naked Egg if you want no added BCAAs. Either costs about the same as good whey and involves no enzymes from any source.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you eat mostly plants or are moving toward a vegan diet, choose soy for the lowest price and a complete profile, or Truvani if lead is your main concern, since it tested under Consumer Reports' level of concern. Take about 30 g of protein per shake to match a whey dose, and consider our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-beginners/">beginner's guide</a> if you are new to protein supplements in general.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, read the full ingredient list for collagen, gelatin, bone broth and carmine, and favor unflavored or minimally flavored powders when vegetarian status matters to you. The protein is rarely the problem; the extras usually are.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming all whey is vegetarian</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most whey comes from cheese, and some cheese is still set with calf rennet. Many vegetarians accept it anyway, but if you do not, an unlabeled tub is an open question rather than a yes. Look for a written brand statement, choose micellar casein or native whey, or switch to egg or plant protein.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming all whey is off limits</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The opposite error costs you the best-value complete protein on the shelf. Most commercial cheese now uses microbial rennet or fermentation-produced chymosin, which are widely accepted as vegetarian, and some brands confirm their process in writing. Ruling out every whey without asking pushes you toward pricier or lower-leucine options for no reason.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Missing collagen in a blend</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Collagen peptides and bone broth are sometimes blended into whey or plant formulas to raise the protein figure cheaply. Both come from animal hides and bones, and collagen is incomplete and low in leucine. Read the full ingredient list, not just the protein source on the front, before you buy a blended powder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking a whey-sized dose of plant protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 25 g scoop of pea or soy delivers noticeably less leucine than 25 g of whey. If plant protein is your main supplement, take around 30 to 35 g per shake, or blend in some whey or egg. Otherwise you may be under-dosing every shake while assuming you are matching a whey user.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a sport certification as a vegetarian label</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport check for banned substances and label accuracy. They say nothing about rennet, gelatin or other animal-derived ingredients. A certified whey can still come from animal-rennet cheese. Only a vegetarian or vegan certification, or a clear brand statement, answers the diet question.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder for vegetarians?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most lacto-ovo vegetarians, a single-ingredient whey such as Naked Whey gives the most leucine per dollar. If you want the rennet question answered in writing, Momentous states its whey is made without rennet. NOW Sports Egg White suits vegetarians who prefer to avoid dairy, and NOW Sports Soy Isolate is the cheapest complete option. All figures are per labels at the time of writing.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey protein vegetarian?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Whey contains no meat, so most vegetarians eat it. The debate is about rennet: whey is a cheese by-product, and some cheese is set with calf rennet. Most cheese now uses microbial or fermentation-produced coagulants, which are widely accepted as vegetarian. Few labels say which was used, so strict vegetarians should look for a brand statement or ask the manufacturer.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is casein protein vegetarian?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Micellar casein is usually separated directly from skim milk by filtration rather than taken from cheese, so rennet is not part of the normal process. That makes it one of the simplest dairy proteins for vegetarians concerned about enzymes. Check the full ingredient list of flavored versions for gelatin or collagen, and confirm with the brand if you need certainty.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is egg white protein vegetarian?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, for ovo and lacto-ovo vegetarians. Egg white powder is dried egg albumen, involves no rennet or cheese making, and is a complete protein with about 2.3 g of leucine per 25 g of protein in the labels we checked. It is not suitable for lacto-vegetarians who avoid eggs, or for vegans.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do vegetarians need protein powder at all?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not necessarily. Lacto-ovo vegetarians can meet typical protein targets of about 1.2 to 1.6 g per kilogram of body weight from eggs, dairy, legumes, tofu and grains. A powder is useful when meals regularly fall short, when you train hard, or when convenience matters. It is a supplement, not a requirement, and not medical advice.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is plant protein enough for a vegetarian building muscle?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, if the dose is right. Plant proteins carry slightly less leucine than whey, so take about 30 to 35 g of protein per shake rather than 25 g, or combine sources. Soy is complete on its own; pea works well blended with rice. Studies at matched leucine show similar muscle gains to whey in resistance-trained adults.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which ingredients make a protein powder non-vegetarian?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Collagen peptides, gelatin, bone broth protein and fish-derived omega-3 are the clear ones. Carmine, a red coloring from insects, appears in some berry flavors. Natural flavors can occasionally be animal-derived without saying so. Unflavored single-ingredient powders, or products with a vegetarian or vegan certification, avoid these questions, so they are the safest default when vegetarian status matters.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

